  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="14e5-72c1-5b30-1303" name="Warring States Chinese (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #18 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). By 400 BC the Zhou dynasty that had succeeded the Shang ruled in name only and a group of seven states vied for power over all China. After nearly two hundred years of warfare the Qin state emerged victorious. Armies of the period were large, well organised and composed largely of infantry, but made full use of all arms and the latest technology, including the deadly crossbow. 

ARMY COMPOSITION:
[Warring States Chinese]
  Artillery: up to 10%
  Characters: up to 25%
  Chariotry and Cavalry: up to 25%
  Infantry: at least 33%
  Characters: up to 25% Chariotry and Cavalry: up to 25% Infantry: At least 33% Artillery: up to 10%</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
